<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!--
    Device art - one plan-view drawing per programmable device, per the icon system of
    docs/design/handoff.md ("device silhouettes, plan view, true proportion") and the card
    anatomy at handoff.md:117 ("abstract key-grid drawing, not a photo").

    Coordinate box: every geometry is authored in the same 92 x 56 box - the dashboard card's art
    frame at 1:1 - with the board centred in it, so in these coordinates the seven are at true
    proportion to one another. Nothing is offset or pre-scaled per device, so a call site may
    either honour the box (draw into a 92 x 56 viewport) and keep that relative scale, or let
    `Stretch="Uniform"` normalise each board to its own bounds and fill the frame - which is what
    the mockups do, their card arts running 68 to 78 units wide inside the same 92-wide frame.

    One geometry, two presentations. The same resource is filled at 92 x 56 for the dashboard
    card and stroked-and-dim at silhouette size (~33 x 20) in the empty-state device picker. The
    call site picks the brush, the stroke and the scale; nothing here carries colour. Because the
    key rects sit inside their body and every subpath winds clockwise, the explicit EvenOdd fill
    rule (the `F0` that opens each path) punches the keys out of the slab when filled - a key grid
    in one colour. At silhouette size that grid collapses into texture, which is why the
    recognition has to survive in the body outlines alone: one piece with a recessed front edge
    vs. two contoured halves vs. two flat halves vs. a single slab vs. three pedals.

    Like Geometry.axaml this is theme-independent - a device does not change shape when the OS
    flips to light - so there are no ThemeDictionaries here.

    Three of the seven have no drawing in docs/design/ and were constructed from the real hardware
    (see docs/app/design-system.md for the deviation log): Advantage2, Freestyle Edge and
    Freestyle Pro. Edge and Edge RGB are the same physical board, so they share a body and differ
    only in the macro column.
  -->

  <!-- Advantage2 - contoured, one piece. The two 4x4 wells are joined by a central bridge in a
       single body, whose front edge carries the recess the real board has between its thumb
       clusters; the fine 12-tab strip across the top is the function/status row the 360 does
       not have. One body against the 360's two is the whole difference at a glance.
       Subpaths, in order: body (1), function strip (12), left well (16), right well (16). -->
